You live in a warm, sunny climate. You spend time outdoors. Yet your blood test shows low vitamin D. Sounds contradictory, right ? But vitamin D deficiency in sunny climates is actually common. In fact, the presence of sunlight does not guarantee adequate vitamin D production.

This blog explores vitamin D deficiency causes using evidence based nutrition course principles to help you understand why deficiency is common even in sunny climates. 
What Is Vitamin D and Why Does It Matter?
Vitamin D is a fat-soluble hormone that regulates calcium and phosphate metabolism. Your body uses it for:
•	Intestinal calcium absorption
•	Bone mineralization
•	Neuromuscular function
•	Skeletal growth
•	Immune function
How does Body Produce Vitamin D?
Your skin produces vitamin D when ultraviolet B (UVB) radiation converts 7-dehydrocholesterol into vitamin D3. Your liver and kidneys then convert it into the active form used by your body. Healthcare providers measure vitamin D status using serum 25(OH)D, the principal biomarker that reflects vitamin D obtained from sunlight and dietary sources. Blood tests quantify these levels to determine your status.
