What Happens When Vitamin D Is Deficient? 
Severe deficiency causes rickets in children and osteomalacia (soft bones) in adults. Less severe deficiency can cause secondary hyperparathyroidism, reduced bone mineral density, muscle weakness, and increased risk of falls. Early detection through blood testing prevents these serious complications.
Sunshine Does Not Guarantee Vitamin D Sufficiency
Abundant sunlight outside does not guarantee your body is producing Vitamin D. Biological synthesis depends on several variables, including solar intensity, exposure duration, time of day, season, skin pigmentation, age, and sunscreen habits.
Beyond biology, daily habits play a major role. Many people living in warm climates remain indoors in air-conditioned homes, offices, or schools to avoid extreme heat. Dense urban settings with tall buildings block direct rays, leaving many tropical residents with virtually zero meaningful UVB exposure during peak hours.
Vitamin D Deficiency Causes: Why You Might Be Deficient Despite Sun
Cause 1: Skin Pigmentation
Understanding Melanin and Vitamin D Absorption Rate
Melanin provides natural protection against ultraviolet radiation but reduces vitamin D synthesis efficiency. People with darker skin require longer sun exposure to produce the same amount of vitamin D as people with lighter skin under identical conditions.
The 3-6x Factor
Some people need 3-6 times longer in the sun to produce the same vitamin D as others. This depends on melanin concentration in the skin. Humans adapted to different UV radiation levels in ancestral environments. In modern settings, this adaptation affects vitamin D production. 
Why Sunny Climates Can Still Lead to Deficiency
Causes of vitamin d deficiency despite sun exposure include:
•	Reduced synthesis efficiency due to melanin concentration
•	Behavioural factors like spending more time indoors in hot climates (heat avoidance)
•	Urban indoor occupations with limited outdoor exposure
•	Protective clothing practices

